/*
Time & Space Complexity

Time: O(n³)

l, r, and i loops

Space: O(n²)

DP table

Problem: Burst Balloons (DP on intervals)
Core idea (key trick)

Don’t think about which balloon to pop first.
Think about which balloon is popped last in a subarray.

This flips the problem from chaotic to deterministic.

Why we add 1 at both ends
newNums[0] = newNums[n+1] = 1;


So that when the last balloon i in [l..r] is popped,
its neighbors are fixed: l-1 and r+1.

This makes the coins gained:

newNums[l-1] * newNums[i] * newNums[r+1]

DP Definition
dp[l][r] = maximum coins obtainable
           by bursting balloons from index l to r (inclusive)

Transition (most important part)

Pick which balloon i is burst last in [l..r]:

dp[l][r] =
max over i in [l..r] of:
    newNums[l-1] * newNums[i] * newNums[r+1]
  + dp[l][i-1]
  + dp[i+1][r]


Why this works:

Left side [l..i-1] is already resolved

Right side [i+1..r] is already resolved

i is last → neighbors are guaranteed

Loop Order (why it’s correct)
for (l = n → 1)
  for (r = l → n)
    for (i = l → r)


Smaller intervals are computed first

dp[l][i-1] and dp[i+1][r] already exist when needed

Classic interval DP ordering.
*/
class Solution {
    public int maxCoins(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] newNums = new int[n+2];
        newNums[0] = newNums[n+1] = 1;
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
            newNums[i+1] = nums[i];
        }
        //dp[l][r] max value acheived if we pop
        // newNums[l] last , we have l to i-1 on left
        // we have l+1 to r on right
        int[][] dp = new int[n+2][n+2];
        for(int l=n;l>=1;l--){
            for(int r = l;r<=n;r++){
                for(int i=l;i<=r;i++){
                    int coins = newNums[l-1]*newNums[i]*newNums[r+1];
                    coins += dp[l][i - 1] + dp[i + 1][r];
                    dp[l][r] = Math.max(dp[l][r], coins);
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[1][n];
    }
}
